---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'LOCATION', title: 'Measurement across your area', text: 'We measure your rank from different points around you. Your rank is read from wherever the customer is standing.' },
  { label: 'PATTERN', title: 'Reading the map', text: 'Strong in the center, weak at the edges? This pattern shows whether the problem is in the profile or on the site.' },
  { label: 'COMPETITORS', title: 'Side by side with competitors', text: 'We take the same measurement for your competitors in the top three. We see in the data which districts they lead in, and why.' },
  { label: 'IMPACT', title: 'Rank isn’t enough', text: 'We read rank together with calls, direction requests and messages. If the phone isn’t ringing, a high rank means nothing.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'tek-nokta', label: 'Looking from one point' },
  { id: 'nasil', label: 'Measurement across your area' },
  { id: 'alan', label: 'The measurement area' },
  { id: 'okuma', label: 'Reading the result' },
  { id: 'rakip', label: 'Competitor comparison' },
  { id: 'aramalar', label: 'Tracked searches' },
  { id: 'sinirlar', label: 'Limits of the measurement' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Isn’t it enough to search from my own phone?',
    a: 'No. The result you see belongs only to the spot where you’re standing, and Google personalizes it based on your search history. To know what your customer sees two districts away, you need measurement across your area.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many searches do you track?',
    a: 'There’s no fixed number. We pick the searches that bring you work; tracking a few of the right searches tells you more than the average of a long list.',
  },
  {
    q: 'If my rank goes up, will I get more customers?',
    a: 'Not by itself. Reaching the top for a search nobody makes won’t make the phone ring; that’s why we read rank together with calls, direction requests and messages.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see my competitors’ rankings too?',
    a: 'Yes. We take the same measurement for your competitors in the top three. You see side by side who leads in which district.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/rank-tracking"
  title="Google Maps Rank Tracking | Wiserrise"
  description="Your rank on the map depends on where the searcher stands. We measure it from different points in your area and read it next to your competitors."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · MAP RANKING"
  h1="We measure your map ranking from every corner of your area."
  crumb="Map Rank Tracking"
  lede="Map rank tracking means measuring your position on Google Maps from different points around you. You don’t have one rank on the map; for the same search you can be second in one district and twentieth in the district across the way. We show on the map where you’re strong and where you’re weak, and read the result next to your competitors."
  serviceName="Google Maps Rank Tracking"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We close the gaps the measurement reveals on your profile and your site.' },
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We manage your category, services, reviews and posts within the rules.' },
    { label: 'GEO', href: p('services/geo'), text: 'We measure whether you’re mentioned in ChatGPT, Gemini and Google’s AI answers.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="tek-nokta">Why is the rank you see on your own phone misleading?</h2>

  <p>
    Because it only shows the rank for the spot where you’re standing. Google ranks map results by the searcher’s location;
    being first in front of your shop doesn’t mean a customer two districts away sees you first too.
  </p>

  <p>
    There’s a trap here as well. The business owner searches from their office, and the point closest to the office is
    where the business is strongest. When a business tells us “I’m already first, and the phone still isn’t ringing”, this
    is the first place we look.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Your own search doesn’t count as measurement.</strong> Google personalizes the result based on your location
      and search history. The rank you see on your phone may not match what your customer sees.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For the same reason we don’t trust “we’re number one” reports that come with a single screenshot. That screenshot shows
    one small corner of the map.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nasil">How does measurement across your area work?</h2>

  <p>
    We take measurements from different points spread around your business. At each point we query the map that a person
    standing there would see on their phone, and record where you rank.
  </p>

  <p>
    The result is a visibility map of your area. Green marks top positions; red marks places where you’re far down the list
    or don’t appear at all. Even without looking at the numbers, you see which district you’re weak in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alan">How do we set the measurement area?</h2>

  <p>
    By the distance your business draws customers from. For a vehicle inspection business that gets customers from nearby
    districts we measure a narrow area; for a law firm that takes clients from all over the city we widen it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Setting the area wrong makes the measurement useless. Set it too narrow and everything looks green, so you relax. Set it
    too wide and everything comes out red, so you learn nothing.
  </p>

  <p>If you have more than one branch, we measure each branch separately around its own location.</p>

  <h2 id="okuma">How do we read the measurement result?</h2>

  <p>
    The real information is in the pattern. Two measurements with the same average can point to completely different
    problems:
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Pattern</th>
          <th>What it tells you</th>
          <th>What we do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Strong in the center, dropping fast toward the edges</td>
          <td>You have no strong signal apart from proximity. The profile and prominence are weak.</td>
          <td>Review count, how up to date the profile is, sources that mention you</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mid-ranking everywhere, in the top three nowhere</td>
          <td>Google doesn’t see you as an exact match for this search.</td>
          <td>Category, service list, the service page on the site</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Strong in one direction, very weak in the opposite one</td>
          <td>There’s a strong competitor on that side.</td>
          <td>A comparison with that competitor, content about that district</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Big differences between neighboring points</td>
          <td>The ranking hasn’t settled yet. The profile is new or changed recently.</td>
          <td>We wait for it to settle and make no big changes in the meantime.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The pattern tells us where to start. Running a review campaign for a profile with a category problem means spending
    effort on the wrong door.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rakip">How do we compare you with your competitors?</h2>

  <p>
    We take the same measurement for your competitors in the top three. That way we look for the answer to “why are they
    ahead?” in the data, and put their profiles side by side with yours:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Primary and additional categories</li>
    <li>Review count, rating and how recent the reviews are</li>
    <li>Service list and business description</li>
    <li>How frequently they add photos and posts</li>
    <li>Which searches their sites show up in</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Once you see the gap, it also becomes clear where the budget should go. If a competitor has several times your
    reviews, we push on the review flow first; if their category is a better fit, we push on the
    <a href={p('services/gbp')}>profile</a> first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aramalar">Which searches do we track?</h2>

  <p>
    The ones that actually bring you work. The average of a long keyword list looks good in a report and tells you nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    We build the list by looking at how customers search for you: “dentist”, “dental implant”, “dentist near me” and so on.
    Your business profile’s data on which searches people find you through, and your site’s Search Console records, shape
    this list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sinirlar">What doesn’t this measurement show?</h2>

  <p>Every measurement has limits, and we tell you about them up front.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rank doesn’t mean revenue.</strong> Being first everywhere for a search nobody makes won’t make the phone
      ring. That’s why we read rank together with profile interactions, meaning the number of calls, direction requests and
      messages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A measurement is a snapshot of that moment.</strong> Google moves local results around during the day as
      well. A shift of two or three places counts as noise; what matters is the direction across measurements taken under
      the same conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On its own, it doesn’t tell you why you dropped.</strong> If you’ve slipped in a district, the cause could be
      a change we made or a competitor getting stronger there. We look at the competitor measurement to tell them apart.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Early measurement on a new profile misleads.</strong> In the first weeks after a profile opens, the ranking
      hasn’t settled. Looking at the measurement in that period and making hasty changes breaks a picture that is
      improving.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts and on the competition. We close the gaps the
    measurement reveals in the <a href={p('services/local-seo')}>local SEO</a> work, and you see what we did and how the
    measurement changed in your weekly report.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>What <span class="highlight">position</span> does your customer see you in?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
